    "body": "Hub Group announced Tuesday that Nasdaq has given it until the end of the year to submit its delayed financial filings. Its shares will continue to trade on the exchange in the interim.\nHub Group ( NASDAQ: HUBG ) submitted a plan to regain compliance at an Oct. 27 hearing. It also asked Nasdaq to stay the delisting action .\nRegaining compliance requires filing its delinquent annual report for last year and quarterly reports for the first three quarters of 2026. The delays stem from an accounting error it discovered in February. The company is also restating results stretching as far back as 2023.\n“The Company’s finance and accounting team continues to work expeditiously to complete the restatement process and file the Delinquent Filings,” a news release stated. “However, the Company cannot confirm that it will be able to complete all of the Delinquent Filings before December 31, 2026.”\nHub Group said it would request another extension if it can’t meet the Dec. 31 deadline, but that a Nasdaq hearings panel has said it only has the authority to grant an extension to Mar. 12.\nEarly last month, Hub announced an overhaul of its board of directors , which included removing three directors and prompting the resignation of three others. The action included the appointment of four new members, including former CFO Thomas White.\nShares of HUBG are off 43% year-to-date compared to the S&P 500, which is up 7%.\nWhy it matters? A potential delisting highlights the severe operational and financial scrutiny Hub Group faces as it works to rectify an accounting error. Successfully filing its financials and completing the restatements are critical for maintaining investor confidence and preserving its public listing status.\nMore FreightWaves articles by Todd Maiden:\nBroker RXO says TL spot rates not entering deflationary phase\nOctober TL rates surge as shipment counts extend positive streak\nForward Air pauses sale of intermodal unit",
